{"name":"Surfer Girl","hex":"#db6484","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"surfer","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A person who rides a surfboard.","A person who surfs the Internet."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/surfer"},{"term":"girl","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A female child.","A woman, especially a young and often attractive woman."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To feminize or girlify; to gender as a girl or as for girls.","To staff with or as a girl or girls."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/girl"}],"entries":[{"title":"Surfer Girl","blocks":[{"text":"Surfer Girl is the third album by the American rock band the Beach Boys, released September 16, 1963 on Capitol Records. It is largely a collection of surf songs. The LP reached number seven in the U.S. and number 13 in the UK. Lead single \"Surfer Girl\", backed with \"Little Deuce Coupe\", was also a top-10 hit. It is also the band's first release with founding member Al Jardine since their debut single, \"Surfin'\"."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/74/SurferGirlCover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Surfer_Girl"},{"title":"Surfer Girl (song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Surfer Girl\" is a song by the American rock band the Beach Boys from their 1963 album Surfer Girl. Written and sung by Brian Wilson, it was released as a single, backed with \"Little Deuce Coupe\", on July 22, 1963. The single was the first Beach Boys record to have Brian Wilson officially credited as the producer."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/c/c0/Surfer_Girl_cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Surfer_Girl_(song)"},{"title":"Surfing","blocks":[{"text":"Surfing is a surface water sport in which an individual, a surfer, uses a board to ride on the forward section, or face, of a moving wave of water, which usually carries the surfer towards the shore. Waves suitable for surfing are primarily found on ocean shores, but can also be found as standing waves in the open ocean, in lakes, in rivers in the form of a tidal bore, or wave pools."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Mavericks_Surf_Contest_2010b.jpg/330px-Mavericks_Surf_Contest_2010b.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Surfing"},{"title":"Girl","blocks":[{"text":"A girl is a young female human, usually a child or an adolescent. While the term girl has other meanings, including young woman, daughter, or girlfriend regardless of age, the first meaning is the most common one."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Young_girl_smiling_in_sunshine_%282%29.jpg/330px-Young_girl_smiling_in_sunshine_%282%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Girl"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/The_Beach_Boys_1962.jpg/500px-The_Beach_Boys_1962.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/The_Beach_Boys_1962.jpg/500px-The_Beach_Boys_1962.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Beach Boys at a March 1963 photoshoot. Here, they abandoned their former Pendleton image in favor of matching black suits.[1] From top: Brian Wilson, Carl Wilson, Dennis Wilson, David Marks, Mike Love","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Beach_Boys_1962.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Surf-riding_1858.jpg/500px-Surf-riding_1858.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb